  "turn": {
    "pericope": "Matthew 22:1-14",
    "refKey": "Matt.22.1-Matt.22.14",
    "gravity": "The day stacks toward one image and one threat. Isaiah's feast on the mountain where death is swallowed up, the table prepared in Psalm 23, and a wedding hall thrown open to the streets — and then, at the very edge of the joy, a guest with no garment and a door shut fast. Grace is a feast freely given, and it lays a claim on the one who comes.",
    "trap": "The text collapses into a dress code: \"make sure you're wearing the right thing,\" and free grace turns into anxious self-policing. The opposite trap is just as easy — \"everyone's invited!\" — which waves the garment away and loses the King's hard question. Both dissolve the tension the parable refuses to drop.",
    "hinge": "The invitation could not be freer — it runs past the worthy to the bad and good alike in the streets — and the King still throws out the man with no garment. Free grace that nonetheless expects you to be changed. You cannot preach this text by dropping either half.",
    "doors": [
      {
        "landing": "Come clothed",
        "claim": "Grace seats you at the table and then asks you to wear it — the feast is free, but it is not indifferent to who you become.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Gregory the Great",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The wedding garment is charity — the love the Lord himself wore when he came to wed the Church to himself. To sit at the feast without it is to be present and still not dressed."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Wesley",
            "tradition": "wesleyan",
            "work": "Explanatory Notes",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The wedding garment is the righteousness of Christ, first imputed, then implanted — given to us, then growing in us."
          },
          {
            "author": "Augustine",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "He goes to the feast without a garment who goes seeking his own honour and not the Bridegroom's."
          }
        ],
        "say": "You are welcome here, and welcome is not the end of it — put on the love you were given.",
        "do": "Send them to examine one place this week where they have come to the table seeking their own honour, and to put on Christ there instead."
      },
      {
        "landing": "The invitation ran to the streets",
        "claim": "The ones first asked would not come, so the doors were thrown open to the crossings of the road — and that is where we were standing.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The first servant sent is Moses, then the Prophets, to those already invited; and the Gentiles were not even in the streets but out in the crossings of the streets, gathered in last."
          },
          {
            "author": "Remigius of Auxerre",
            "tradition": "medieval",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "Those first bidden — the people who thought the feast was theirs — were found not worthy, and the hall was filled from the roads instead."
          }
        ],
        "say": "This feast reached you because grace refuses an empty hall — you are the unlikely guest, gathered from the crossroads.",
        "do": "Move them from astonishment at being included to widening the table — naming who is still out in the crossings of their own town."
      },
      {
        "landing": "A hall of bad and good",
        "claim": "The wedding hall this side of the end holds bad with good together, and the garment-inspection belongs to the King, not to the guests.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Gregory the Great",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "By the wedding-feast is meant the present Church, into which some enter who shall yet perish; in this present Church there cannot be bad without good, nor good without bad — and he is not good who refuses to endure the bad."
          },
          {
            "author": "Origen",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The marriage-feast is filled when those gathered by the Apostles sit down — but it behoved that both bad and good be gathered in, until the King himself comes in to see the guests."
          }
        ],
        "say": "You are sitting in a mixed hall, and your job is not to inspect your neighbour's garment but to wear your own and trust the King to sort the feast.",
        "do": "Turn them from policing the hall to patient love within it — one concrete way to \"endure the bad\" without leaving the table."
      }
    ],
    "subtract": "These three are doors, not points — mutually exclusive landings (a changed life / astonished welcome / patient humility). Walk through one and let the other two go; the choosing is the sermon's focus.",
    "source": "Catena Aurea (Aquinas; J.H. Newman tr., public domain) + Wesley's Explanatory Notes, retrieved from ~/reception-corpus — attested, not authored.",
